he graph on its cases has no cycle, the pairwise check being necessary and not sufficient, with a forbidden and two distinguished continuations; it allows and does not rank, sits upstream of the survivor rule, and the record has no decision object to read a case from. Follow-up of 2026-11-09, on finite memory: of four further matters one fits and three fit in part. The holding fits in part: permission reads a case base only through the reach of its holding — the transitive closure of the cases' decisions, with covering — so the cases may be forgotten and the holding kept, and over a finite set of forms it is eventually constant; over one pair of kinds, n strengths carry a different holding for each set of steps, so the kinds of reasons alone give no finite representation. Decisions on the record fit in part: an assessed act — grounds, surviving grounds against, the assessor's verdict fixing which side won — is a decision object beside the docket, conduct can be held to the holding of its own assessed past, and a verdict against a choice that past backs leaves nothing permitted; the constraint still allows and does not rank. Forgetting fits: one Fourier–Motzkin step computes the projection that drops a coordinate, forgetting is exact and conservative on the retained fragment, a settled coordinate may be forgotten under every later docket state and settlement and a closed account keeps its receipts and its denotation, while forgetting an unsettled coordinate can cost the later forced interval, [3/4, 1] against [0, 1]; loss relative to a reference schedule composes as slack and is bounded when summable; and a reasoner choosing what to drop after seeing the record obtains [0, 1] where each coordinate fixed in advance leaves [3/4, 1] on one record. A vocabulary change as an act on the docket fits in part: with a declared protected fragment its licence is a refinement certificate and its challenge a broken-commitment witness with decidable validity, a licensed change admi